# PERF-HIST-SUB-LOSSLESS A/B bench — 2026-06-10
# Implementation: parent-histogram subtraction in modular/tree_learn.rs
#   (issue #64 chunk 1, spec: benchmarks/perf_hist_sub_2026-06-10.meta)
# Implementation commit: 276c94dd578d (bench ran binaries built from it
#   vs its parent 34f0cde1 as base). Host: lilith (7950X, 128 GB, WSL2).
# No -C target-cpu=native. 6 iters/side, interleaved, median reported.
# Binaries: {base,ours}-default = default CLI features, 1T cells via
#   --threads 1; {base,ours}-ptl adds --features parallel-tree-learning
#   for the 8T cells.
#
# PRIMARY GATE (lossless photos, 5 CLIC-1024 x e{7,9} x {1T,8T}):
#   20/20 cells faster; mean -20.7%; every cell bytes_identical=True.
#   e7 1T: -14.8..-33.7%  e9 1T: -10.4..-23.3%
#   e7 8T:  -9.9..-28.3%  e9 8T:  -5.6..-38.1%
#   Gate ">=10% on >=2 cells": PASS (17/20 cells >= 10%).
#
# NO-REGRESSION GUARD (7-cell lossy set): the in-grid lossy numbers ran
#   under heavy concurrent agent load and are noise-dominated (e.g.
#   imac_dark e5 showed +10.7% with sample spreads 934..1188 / 933..1215).
#   Quiet-machine re-run (load <4, 6 iters/side, same binaries):
#     imac_dark_e5 d=1.0: -0.50%   clic097_e7 d=1.0: +0.86%
#   Both within noise; terminal e6 -0.1% in-grid. Guard: PASS.
#   (Lossy cells cannot reach the tensor path: e<8 modular tree only runs
#   for the patches ref frame; bytes_identical=True on all 27 cells.)
#
# RSS (gate g) — DISCLOSED OVERAGE: imac_dark e7 lossless peak RSS
#   2,573,984 -> 2,622,828 KB = +47.7 MB (+1.9%). The brief's +20 MB
#   number came from the e7 CLIC-photo estimate (~15 MB transient);
#   imac_dark's wider/deeper screenshot tree caches more levels. The
#   cost is structural-bounded (parent tensors freed at derivation,
#   active-branch depth only). If +48 MB is unacceptable, raising the
#   weighted_total >= 2048 profitability gate shrinks cached depth.
#
# ── ADDENDUM (2026-06-10, post-landing): RSS overage ROOT-CAUSED — it is
#    glibc adaptive-arena measurement noise, NOT live tensor memory. ──────
#
# The single-sample +47.7 MB above sits inside a ±120 MB run-to-run band.
# Same binaries, same cell (imac_dark e7 lossless), /usr/bin/time -v:
#
#   Unpinned glibc (default), n=8/side alternating, sorted KB:
#     base: 2432720 2472020 2500808 2517208 2577480 2602492 2652916 2684204
#     ours: 2466916 2495920 2501280 2587292 2619164 2623668 2639184 2681820
#     medians: base 2,547,344 / ours 2,603,228 (+54.6 MB) — but the
#     distributions overlap almost entirely (base max 2,684,204 KB is
#     ABOVE ours max 2,681,820 KB); per-binary spread is ±120 MB.
#
#   GLIBC_TUNABLES=glibc.malloc.mmap_threshold=131072 (adaptive threshold
#   growth disabled), n=4/side alternating, KB:
#     base: 1934644 1939040 1934112 1910252   (median 1,934,378)
#     ours: 1938924 1923600 1931556 1932696   (median 1,932,126)
#     => OURS is -2.2 MB vs BASE, and noise collapses to ±10 MB. Both
#     binaries also drop ~600 MB of arena retention.
#
# Mechanism: the whole encoder (base included) churns multi-MB buffers;
# glibc's adaptive mmap_threshold reacts by retaining freed arena pages,
# so unpinned peak RSS is dominated by allocator policy, varying ±120 MB
# between runs of the SAME binary. With the policy pinned, the tensor
# live set is invisible at MB scale — consistent with the structural
# bound (~2.3 MB/tensor on this cell x the 2-3 chained levels its
# derive_pays gate permits; parents freed at derivation).
#
# Consequence: gate (g) "+20 MB" PASSES on the apples-to-apples
# measurement (-2.2 MB median, pinned). Do NOT raise the
# weighted_total >= 2048 gate for memory reasons — there is no memory
# cost to shrink, and raising it would cut the wall win.
#
# (Count nit, same TSV data: 18/20 cells are >= 10.0% — the -10.38% e9
# 1T cell qualifies; only -9.91% and -5.61% fall short.)
#
# Commands (addendum):
#   for r in 1..8: /usr/bin/time -v {base,ours} imac_dark.png out.jxl --lossless -e 7   # alternating
#   GLIBC_TUNABLES=glibc.malloc.mmap_threshold=131072 <same loop, n=4/side>
# Addendum host: lilith (7950X, 128 GB, WSL2), binaries from c19815ff-
# equivalent tree (276c94dd) vs its base, identical to the table above.
